  "name": "Tan singing (Indo-Caribbean local classical song)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Indo-Caribbean local classical song of Trinidad and Guyana, developed by descendants of indentured labourers from North Indian light-classical and devotional song genres and sung at weddings and in competitions. A solo singer is accompanied by harmonium, dholak and dhantal (a struck steel rod), and the name comes from the tan, the virtuosic melismatic runs the singer improvises; Peter Manuel's study documents it.",
